You write with a great deal of certainty, but as the poster may have
discovered already, mileage may vary.  Personally, I did a fair amount of
saving for my first few months of playing, and it helped me quite a bit.
I still learned about the things that could kill me, but they didn't
necessarily set me back to level 1 every time.  That meant I had more
opportunities to interact with identified items and powerful monsters,
both of which are important to know about but rather rare at low levels.

Plus, and this is a separate issue, I think it was more fun.  I have
rather mixed feelings about Nethack's philosophy of making it easy for a
single mistake to blast away hours of playing.  It's all well and good
once you're aware of the potential mistakes and thus have a fighting
chance to avoid them and/or recover, but for a moderately experienced
player it can just be frustrating to have your best game wiped out without
warning because you stood near a drawbridge or put a wand in a bag.  Some
people can just shrug and start from scratch, but for others, "restore and
don't do that again" will keep them playing the game longer.

I gather your experience has been different, but for me savescumming did
have a useful place.

